-- Sched.bang — cooperative scheduling as an ORDINARY user effect (ADR-0101 §G1).
--
-- A scheduler is a HANDLER, not a language feature: this file declares one effect
-- with two ops and stops there. Every scheduling POLICY (round-robin, depth-first,
-- seeded-random) is a `with Sched as sched { … }` block at the use site — see
-- ../sched-roundrobin/main.bang vs. ../sched-swap-dfs/main.bang for the same
-- driver body under two different installed handlers.
--
-- NOT a bundled `std/` module (ADR-0104's `stdModules` table in Main.lean):
-- registering a new bundled name is a Main.lean/driver change, out of this lane's
-- write scope (a LIBRARY-CODE lane touches only .bang files). `import Sched`
-- resolves this file via the ORDINARY same-dir-then-root file probe
-- (`resolveModulePath`, ADR-0093 D1) — no compiler change needed. See
-- ../../docs/notes/sched-library-demo.md §"why a plain file, not std/".
--
-- `spawn : Int -> Int` — register a task, argument/result both carry a caller-chosen
-- task id (v1 has no dynamic id allocator in a pure handler clause without carried
-- state, ADR-0092 D5 open; the id is supplied by the caller and echoed back, which
-- is enough for a handler to observe/veto every spawn — e.g. a resource-limited
-- handler could refuse ids past a cap by returning a sentinel, though none of the
-- demos here exercise refusal).
--
-- `next : Int -> Int` — the scheduling decision. The driver calls `sched.next(round)`
-- once per round, passing a ROUND COUNTER (not a mutable "whose turn" register — the
-- handler param is read-only in v1, ADR-0095 D4/ADR-0092 D5, so state cannot live in
-- the handler; see the design note's §"stateless seed-splitting"). The handler
-- returns which runnable task index advances next; the DRIVER interprets the number
-- (0/1/2 for a 3-task queue here). A policy is a PURE function of the round counter —
-- round-robin: `round mod N`; depth-first: the constant `0`; seeded: an LCG of the
-- round — three different pure functions, three different runtimes, one effect.
pub effect Sched { spawn : Int -> Int, next : Int -> Int }
